#{[17592186233937 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233937 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233937 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233937 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/09/09ea9b27d573f7ca318e00a912a18d6c8de8b66ead5c85fb90ebea7f5bd20ca8.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(2\\sqrt{3},-6),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233937 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a8a7118-dcfe-5423-982d-0747799e649d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233937 #edb/ref 1074 "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{′}+\\theta =2\\pi $\n\n![](images/eb/eb659af0765e200333ca35b716772d92765c7f46ccc4b267243b3c82ba88a1e8.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{6}{2\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta +{\\theta }^{′}=2\\pi ,$ we get $\\theta =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233966 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233966 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233966 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233966 #edb/ref 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{y+3}-2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233966 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8bdad77-9abf-5122-8282-321bd75ae69b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233966 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{y+3}+2.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{y+3}+2,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{y+3}-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{y+3}-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{y+3}+2}{\\sqrt{y+3}+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5(\\sqrt{y+3}+2)}{(\\sqrt{y+3}-2)(\\sqrt{y+3}+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{y+3}+10}{(\\sqrt{y+3})^{2}-{2}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{y+3}+10}{y+3-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{y+3}+10}{y-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234060 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234060 #edb/ref 1069 "q-45468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234060 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234060 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following transformations map every polygon to a congruent polygon?\n\nI. $(x,y)↦(x-1,y)$\n\nII. $(x,y)↦(-y,x)$\n\nIII. $(x,y)↦(2x,y)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234060 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7b38bd8-148f-55bd-b6dc-69c04541be76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234060 #edb/ref 1074 "A rigid motion is a transformation that preserves distances. There are three types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n\n- Rotations\n\n- Reflections\n\nAlso, two polygons are congruent if and only if one can be mapped to another using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is a translation of $1$ unit to the right. This is a rigid motion. So, it maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n\n- Transformation II is a rotation of ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the origin. This is a rigid motion. So, it maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n\n- Transformation III is a stretch with a stretch factor of $2$ in the $x$-direction. This is *not* a rigid motion. So, it does not map every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234061 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234061 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46167" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234061 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234061 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $y=4{x}^{2}-24x+24$ can be written as $y=a(x+b)^{2}+c.$ Calculate $a+b+c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234061 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b10f970-f0c0-5c1c-948f-9f92d7941265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234061 #edb/ref 1074 "To express the equation of the parabola in vertex form, we need to complete the square. \n\nFirst, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-24x+24 & =4({x}^{2}-6x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+6 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+6 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+6 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2})-{3}^{2}+6 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+6 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+6 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}-6x+6) & = \\\\ 4((x-3)^{2}-3) & = \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,  $a+b+c=4+(-3)+(-12)=-11.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234078 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449382 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186234078 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449383 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186234078 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449384 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186234078 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234078 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234078 #edb/ref 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\n- **II.** $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\n- **III.** $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186234078 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6cc47f7-3bf2-54bf-a0ea-9009aac01bb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234078 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P-Q={x}^{3}-{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{7}={x}^{3+7}={x}^{10},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234083 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234083 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234083 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234083 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ when $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234083 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "daf077a1-46a3-5bb7-a73f-ea41666a8c97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234083 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing the radical as a rational exponent, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2/5}}={x}^{-2/5}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with $n=-\\frac{2}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2/5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{-2/5-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{-7/5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5{x}^{7/5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234105 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234105 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234105 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234105 #edb/ref 1071 "To measure the success of an amusement park, Ed uses the following three functions that model the daily number of visitors to the park $t$ minutes after the park is opened to the public.\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of people inside the park\n\n- $E(t)$ - the rate at which people enter the park\n\n- $L(t)$ - the rate at which people leave the park\n\nWhat is the relationship between the functions above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234105 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c00a28ec-ae50-52ff-8f5b-35e535d7e4c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234105 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of people inside the park $t$ minutes after opening. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }N}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of people inside the park changes $t$ minutes after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of people inside the park at time $t$ by finding the difference between the entry and exit rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)-L(t).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234137 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=1$ and ${a}_{2}=6,$ then ${a}_{4}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19fe87cd-0d79-5f77-b53f-849ad0d5ffc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234137 #edb/ref 1074 "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =6+1 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2+2} & ={a}_{2+1}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{3}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =7+6 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${a}_{4}=13.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234141 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234141 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53708" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234141 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234141 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following transformations map any polygon to a similar polygon?\n\nI. Stretching with a stretch factor of $2$ in the $x$-direction and a stretch factor of $4$ in the $y$-direction\n\nII. Translation by $3$ units to the left and $4$ units upward\n\nIII. Dilation of scale factor $2$ with center $O,$ then reflection across the $y$-axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234141 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "384ee4e4-0b37-56f7-a3a5-86d23f2e43a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234141 #edb/ref 1074 "A similarity transformation is any transformation made up of rigid motions (rotations, reflections, and translations) and dilations.\n\nA similarity transformation will map any polygon to a similar polygon.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I includes stretching with different factors in the $x$- and $y$-direction. Thus, it is not a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation II is a combination of two translations, which are rigid motions. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation III is a combination of dilation of scale factor $2$ and a reflection. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234163 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234163 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234163 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234163 #edb/ref 1071 "The third term of a geometric sequence is $108$ and its fifth term is $3.$  Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234163 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "727e7006-93ff-5e5c-a29e-52adc22e5960" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234163 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{5}=3$ and ${a}_{3}=108.$ Substituting $n=5$ and $m=3$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{5-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{108} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234168 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234168 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234168 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234168 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$ in terms of a reference angle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234168 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c210cee-fd55-5399-a645-1646a3c5f4b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234168 #edb/ref 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={126}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{7\\pi }{10}$ is equivalent to ${126}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/89/89b5e2656378adb5d91c9f58c261e4beaae252b67819b904bf209f6ed8a5a25e.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{10}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{7\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$ must be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent) is always negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234200 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234200 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234200 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234200 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)=2x+6$ and $g(x)=\\frac{x}{2}+k.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234200 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1766a0cc-97ef-5939-9ca2-60e65363339c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234200 #edb/ref 1074 "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =\\frac{x}{2}+k \\\\ g(f(x)) & =\\frac{f(x)}{2}+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=2x+6$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =\\frac{f(x)}{2}+k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+6}{2}+k \\\\  & =x+3+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}+3+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ 3+k & =0 \\\\ k & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234209 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440294 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186234209 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440295 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186234209 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440296 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186234209 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234209 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234209 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=|x-4|+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty ,3)$\n- **II.** $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-\\infty ,5]$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186234209 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "912ff428-058e-5197-af7a-fcbe7f3d6387" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234209 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-4|+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $4$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-4|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x-4|$ by $2$ units up to get $f(x)=|x-4|+2.$\n\n![](images/56/56956d0f23ae6828098507712fd00525dc4f114a195716dc16e63d8475e9c143.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,3).$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in (1,\\infty )$  and $(-\\infty ,5].$ On each of these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234216 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234216 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6690" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234216 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234216 #edb/ref 1071 "Is the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-2x$ even, odd, or neither?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234216 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48b449a7-d277-5309-976b-1d1765d56b87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234216 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =2(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne f(x),$ the function is not even.\n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(2{x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ the function is odd." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234235 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234235 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234235 #edb/ref 1071 "Given an arithmetic sequence that has ${a}_{4}=13$ and ${a}_{8}=41,$ what is the index of the term $90?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234235 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51d856f6-bec1-5419-b978-851c105050a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234235 #edb/ref 1074 "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term ${a}_{1}.$\n\nSince we are given ${a}_{4}=13$ and ${a}_{8}=41,$ we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{4}}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{41-13}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{4} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{4}=13$ is $3$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+3d \\\\ 13 & ={a}_{1}+3(7) \\\\ 13 & ={a}_{1}+21 \\\\ -8 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-8+(n-1)(7) \\\\  & =-8+7n-7 \\\\  & =7n-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=90,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =7n-15 \\\\ 90 & =7n-15 \\\\ 105 & =7n \\\\ \\frac{105}{7} & =n \\\\ 15 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $90$ is the $15$th term of the sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234270 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234270 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234270 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{t+1}{t(t+2)}-\\frac{1}{3t}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234270 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ee038b53-750c-5b8c-99dc-a961b717435d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234270 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $3t(t+2),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $3$, and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $(t+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t+1}{t(t+2)}-\\frac{1}{3t} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{t+1}{t(t+2)}-\\frac{t+2}{t+2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(t+1)}{3t(t+2)}-\\frac{t+2}{3t(t+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(t+1)-(t+2)}{3t(t+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3t+3-t-2}{3t(t+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2t+1}{3t(t+2)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234280 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234280 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234280 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234280 #edb/ref 1071 "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=6\\sqrt{x}+5x-13$ at the point $(1,-2)$ intersects the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234280 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43b75bde-30a1-5aa7-ad2b-9b63b76aaef0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234280 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)=6\\sqrt{x}+5x-13.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\sqrt{x}+5x-13) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{1/2}+5x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{-1/2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=3(1)^{-1/2}+5=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =8(x-1) \\\\ y & =8x-8-2 \\\\ y & =8x-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =8x-10 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(\\frac{5}{4},0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234325 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234325 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234325 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234325 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4c/4ce20d9d732816efda0a2fd3aebabc467f9f2c73d0b6037f4ad8f95d8e8ef1e7.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234325 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a33798d-52c0-57f6-a233-6585edd299f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234325 #edb/ref 1074 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/6e/6e070d26915bcb11118f5a23f36b80038d5dd865e32a8517a12c10939ca02f0b.png)\n\nFirst, we find the area of $△ABD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{85}^{∘} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{85}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈23.908\\,7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area of $△BCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BCD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 14\\cdot 16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{36.4}^{∘} \\\\  & =112\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{36.4}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈66.462\\,9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BCD} \\\\  & ≈23.908\\,7+66.462\\,9 \\\\  & ≈90.371\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $90.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234470 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234470 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88472" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234470 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234470 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}=3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234470 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f70c750-2bdb-5272-875c-7d304eb180d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234470 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x} & =3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}-3{e}^{5x} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}+4x-3){e}^{5x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{5x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x+3)(2x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{5x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{5x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},$ and their product is $(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234471 #edb/ref 1069 "q-889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234471 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234471 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $|z|$ if $z=2-3\\text{i}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234471 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d30493c3-51f6-5c83-b546-665bbdac5511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234471 #edb/ref 1074 "For a complex number $z=a+b\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFor the complex number $z=2-3\\text{i},$ we have $a=2$ and $b=-3.$ So, the magnitude is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}|2-3\\text{i}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234483 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234483 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234483 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234483 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=3\\sqrt[6]{3x+13}-6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234483 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aebcd2ff-63a9-5afc-9df9-bf4ebc2f269f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234483 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[6]{3x+13}-6=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt[6]{3x+13}-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\sqrt[6]{3x+13} & =6 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{3x+13} & =2 \\\\ (\\sqrt[6]{3x+13})^{6} & ={2}^{6} \\\\ 3x+13 & =64 \\\\ 3x & =51 \\\\ x & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt[6]{3\\cdot 17+13}-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\sqrt[6]{64}-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2-6 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=17.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234488 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${log}_{8}⁡(32)$ without using a calculator." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9bbddd9a-4e6f-51fe-9ece-2d2a83dcd62f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234488 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ and $X=32.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡(32) & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(32)}{{log}_{2}⁡(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{5})}{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234512 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234512 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234512 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\sqrt[4]{16}=2$ is equivalent to" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32b42bfe-7421-54f2-832b-a9a8c0cbc5ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234512 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n{16}^{1/4}=2\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${16}^{1/4}=2$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=2,b=16$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${log}_{16}⁡2=\\frac{1}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245880 #edb/ref 1080 "(3{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(3m-n)^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245880 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245880 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "af74e506-fa73-5d0f-bbac-5b9065d0746a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245890 #edb/ref 1080 "(3{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(m+n)(m-n)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245890 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245890 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b1441774-55dd-55f5-a7c2-50018f1c1cc2" #edb/